---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Gallery from '../components/Gallery.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import QuoteForm from '../components/QuoteForm.astro';
import { projectsIn } from '../lib/media';
import { serviceSchema, breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/homestead-pergolas/';
const title = 'Pergolas Homestead FL | Custom Pergola Builders';
const description =
  'Pergolas built for Homestead backyards — wide, open, treeless lots with full sun and real wind exposure. Aluminium structures sized for the span and the conditions.';

const crumbs = [{ href: '/service-areas/', label: 'Service Areas' }, { label: 'Homestead' }];

const schema = [
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Pergola design and installation in Homestead, Florida',
    description,
    path,
    serviceType: 'Pergola construction',
    areaServed: ['Homestead, Florida', 'Miami-Dade County, Florida'],
    offers: ['Custom pergolas', 'Aluminum pergolas', 'Freestanding pool structures', 'Outdoor kitchen covers'],
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs, path),
  webPageSchema({ path, name: title, description }),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} crumbs={crumbs} schema={schema}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Homestead"
    title="Pergolas in Homestead: Shade Where There Is None"
    lede="Homestead has the opposite problem to central Miami. The lots are wide, flat and generous — and almost completely unshaded. New subdivisions come with a lawn, a slab and nothing tall enough to break the sun, which is why a structure here is usually the first shade the property has ever had."
    facts={[
      { label: 'Typical lot', value: 'Newer subdivision, wide and open, little mature planting' },
      { label: 'Typical build', value: 'Larger freestanding structures with real span' },
      { label: 'Design driver', value: 'Full-day sun and open-field wind exposure' },
      { label: 'Common extras', value: 'Outdoor kitchens, screening and ceiling fans' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>What is different about building here</h2>
      <p>
        The defining feature of a Homestead backyard is exposure. On the newer developments
        spreading south and west of the city, houses go up faster than landscaping matures,
        which leaves a lot of properties with sun on the deck from mid-morning until it sets.
        There is no borrowed shade from a neighbour's oak and nothing breaking the wind coming
        across the open ground and the farmland beyond it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That changes the design in two concrete ways. First, the structure has to be big
        enough to matter — a small cover on a wide, bright lot creates a patch of shade that
        nobody uses, and the honest recommendation is usually larger than people first ask
        for. Second, wind is a genuine design load rather than a footnote. On an open lot the
        structure sees more of it than the same structure would tucked between houses in the
        city, and that flows straight through to footing size and anchor specification.
      </p>
      <p>
        The upside is that Homestead lots are the easiest ones in South Florida to build on.
        Access is usually straightforward, there is room to stage materials, and post
        positions are rarely constrained by anything except the pool. When a yard gives you
        that much freedom, the design can be about what you want rather than what fits.
      </p>

      <h2>Scale is the decision</h2>
      <p>
        With space available, the question stops being "will it fit" and becomes "how much of
        the yard should this cover". A structure that shades a dining table is a different
        proposition from one that covers a table, a lounge area, a grill run and part of the
        pool deck — and on a Homestead lot both are physically possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Larger spans are exactly where{' '}
        <a href="/pergolas/aluminum/">aluminium</a> earns its keep: slimmer members clear more
        distance, which means fewer posts standing in the middle of an otherwise open yard.
        Combining roof types also becomes worthwhile at this size — a solid insulated section
        over the kitchen and seating, an open or louvered section over the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>Sun, wind and what to build in</h2>
      <p>
        On an unshaded lot, two additions do more than anything else for how usable the space
        actually is. <strong>Ceiling fans</strong> under a solid roof move enough air to change
        how the space feels in August, and they need blocking designed into the structure
        rather than an afterthought. <strong>Screening</strong> on the western side — fixed
        slats or roll-down screens — is what makes the space usable at five in the afternoon
        rather than after sunset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Newer Homestead developments frequently have an HOA design review covering anything
        visible from the street or from neighbouring properties. Pull the guidelines before
        the design gets far; colour and height constraints are easy to work with early and
        expensive to discover late.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sand">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our work</p>
        <h2>Structures built for open lots</h2>
      </div>
      <Gallery projects={projectsIn('outdoor-living')} limit={2} />
      <p style="margin-top:var(--space-l)">
        <a class="btn btn-dark" href="/gallery/">See every project</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell form-band">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Get a pergola quote in Homestead</h2>
        <p>
          Photographs of the yard and rough dimensions of the area you want covered are
          enough to start. Say which way the back of the house faces — on an open lot that
          single fact changes the design more than anything else you can tell us.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also see <a href="/kendall-pergolas/">Kendall</a>,{' '}
          <a href="/miami-pergolas/">Miami</a> and the full{' '}
          <a href="/service-areas/">service areas list</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QuoteForm service="Pergola" city="Homestead" compact />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="A yard with nothing but sun in it?"
    body="That is the easiest kind of project to get right — and the one where a structure makes the biggest difference."
    secondaryLabel="Pergola options"
    secondaryHref="/pergolas/"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .form-band { display: grid; gap: var(--space-l); }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .form-band { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-2xl); align-items: start; }
  }
</style>
